Traction, stress and local force balance (@mech-stress-field: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @mechanical-engineering, #source "../sources/Mechanics-of-Materials-Roylance.lingua#r_7K2RYA9504FGFGJTVWHMWXD5WY", #source "../sources/Applied-Mechanics-of-Solids-Bower.lingua#r_109WFZBWMEMGHV9TGFD22H2F1W", #mechanical-engineering "Principal-stresses-rotated-axes-and-invariants.lingua#r_2AVTQVMWSHVEAER99D61KQZNM8", #materials-engineering "Elastic-constitutive-laws-and-the-assumptions-they-carry.lingua#r_7PD9HSJTERFS77Q3TRSX6CAVP1") { r_0YN63QG0NYS92FCEFQBT14BY64
**Read:** [Modules in Mechanics of Materials — David Roylance](../sources/Mechanics-of-Materials-Roylance.lingua#r_7K2RYA9504FGFGJTVWHMWXD5WY), Equilibrium; [Applied Mechanics of Solids — Allan F. Bower](../sources/Applied-Mechanics-of-Solids-Bower.lingua#r_109WFZBWMEMGHV9TGFD22H2F1W), sections 2.3–2.4. Reuse the free-body reasoning at an increasingly small boundary.

Traction is force per area acting across a surface with a specified unit normal n. In a classical continuum its vector is t = σ n. Normal stress is n·t; the remainder is tangential traction. A stress tensor describes the dependence on surface orientation, not six unrelated loads.

With σ = [[100,30,0],[30,20,0],[0,0,0]] MPa, a surface normal in the x direction has normal traction 100 MPa and y-directed shear traction 30 MPa. Opposite sides of an internal cut carry equal-and-opposite traction because their outward normals reverse.

Local force balance is div σ + b = ρa, with body force b per volume. For static equilibrium the right side is zero. Angular-momentum balance gives a symmetric stress tensor in the ordinary continuum without independent couple stresses. This assumption is part of the model, not a rule to impose on every possible microstructured theory.

**Work:** Derive equilibrium of a small rectangular volume, keeping the area and volume orders. Integrate a traction distribution over a section and recover its force and moment resultants. Distinguish applied surface traction, body force, prescribed displacement and the reaction inferred from enforcing that displacement.

